{
  "abstract": "We give two finite coefficient-pigeonhole proofs of uniform prime inequalities for imaginary quadratic characters. For a negative fundamental discriminant $-D$, let $C_\\chi(x)=\\sum_{p\\le x}\\chi_{-D}(p)\\log p/p$. Our elementary quadratic construction proves, for every integer $K\\ge16$ and real $x\\ge1$,\n$C_\\chi(x)>-\\frac{\\log x}{K-3}-\\left(\\frac{K-2}{2(K-3)}+\\frac1{K+1}\\right)\\log D-4\\log K-26$,\nwithout a class-number assumption. Selective integer scaling restores the local Frobenius divisibility of directional Hasse jets. Strong induction on smaller inert-prime cutoffs pays the scaling height, while an established finite-field root count supplies nonvanishing. Every induction constant and the base range are explicit. A second construction uses a three-embedding group and Nakamaye's classical multiplicity estimate. A matched pair of integer coordinates controls planes containing the derivative direction, permitting evaluation height $64\\sqrt{KT}D^{1/4}$ with a local derivative loss below $4/K^2$. This gives a different all-cutoff prime profile. Positive-part integration supplies the logarithmic-derivative interface for either bound. Combining the quadratic induction with principal-norm counts proves $h(-D)=1\\Rightarrow D<100000$, with $\\log(100000)=11.512925464970228\\ldots$, using no algebraic-group zero estimate in that route. A standalone CPU replay checks exact support identities, local coefficients, finite-field ranks, induction constants, and the rational endpoint margin. The contribution is the complete finite synthesis and comparison of these mechanisms; the Frobenius and nonvanishing principles are imported. The bounds are not claimed to improve the best determinant results. No new general zero estimate, census, or classification is asserted.",
